{"birds": [{"name": "Carolina Chickadee", "sci": "Poecile carolinensis", "n": 8, "first": "9:03 AM", "last": "11:33 AM", "ts": 1793801017, "mins": [543, 544, 544, 560, 561, 561, 692, 693], "anim": null, "trunk": false, "fact": "Chickadees hide seeds in many separate spots and can remember where for weeks, then dig them back out when food gets scarce.", "vox": "sounds/voice/poecile-carolinensis.mp3?v=1793113028", "snd": "sounds/poecile-carolinensis.mp3?v=1793113078", "sndby": "J.R. Rigby, xeno-canto XC309925, CC BY-NC-SA 4.0", "key": "chickadee", "cm": 12.5, "img": "cutouts/chickadee.png", "w": 686, "h": 800, "len": 829.0, "beak": [0.78, 0.05, "R"], "mask": {"c": 32, "r": 37, "b": "AAB+AQAA/8MAAf+DAAP/gwAD/wYAA/8GAAf/PAAH/7wAB//8AA//+AAf//4AH/+/AD//vwA//38Af/98AP//5AD//sAA//3AAf/9gAH//4AD//+AA///gAf//4APg/wADgH8ABwA+AA4APgAeAH4AHAB8ADgA/AAwAeAAMAPAAAAHwAAADwAAAA8AAAAeAAAADAAAA=="}}, {"name": "House Finch", "sci": "Haemorhous mexicanus", "n": 64, "first": "9:03 AM", "last": "12:23 PM", "ts": 1793801022, "mins": [543, 544, 544, 545, 545, 546, 546, 546, 547, 548, 548, 549, 549, 550, 550, 551, 551, 551, 551, 552, 553, 553, 554, 554, 555, 555, 556, 559, 559, 560, 560, 561, 562, 562, 563, 563, 563, 564, 572, 584, 653, 683, 684, 684, 685, 685, 686, 686, 686, 687, 687, 687, 688, 693, 694, 699, 700, 732, 741, 741, 742, 742, 743, 743], "anim": null, "trunk": false, "fact": "Native to the West, house finches were released on Long Island in 1940 as pet-shop “Hollywood finches” and spread across the East. Redder males are favored by females.", "vox": "sounds/voice/haemorhous-mexicanus.mp3?v=1793113005", "snd": "sounds/haemorhous-mexicanus.mp3?v=1793113108", "sndby": "Ted Floyd, xeno-canto XC622745, CC BY-NC-SA 4.0", "key": "housefinch", "cm": 14.0, "img": "cutouts/housefinch.png", "w": 800, "h": 581, "len": 662.0, "beak": [0.12, 0.13, "L"], "mask": {"c": 32, "r": 23, "b": "B+AAAAfwAQoP8AHOH/gB7wf8Af8D/gH/B/8A3wf/AP4H/4zgB//fwAP/zwAD//4AAf/+AAD//AAAf/gAAD/8AA/+/AAH4B4AB+APAB6AD4A4AAeA8AAAAMAAAAA="}}, {"name": "White-breasted Nuthatch", "sci": "Sitta carolinensis", "n": 2, "first": "9:04 AM", "last": "9:05 AM", "ts": 1793801049, "mins": [544, 545], "anim": null, "trunk": true, "fact": "Nuthatches climb down tree trunks head first. The name comes from “nut-hack”: they wedge a seed into bark and hack it open.", "vox": "sounds/voice/sitta-carolinensis.mp3?v=1793113050", "snd": "sounds/sitta-carolinensis.mp3?v=1793113095", "sndby": "Andrew Spencer, xeno-canto XC14860, CC BY-NC-ND 2.5", "key": "nuthatch", "cm": 14.0, "img": "cutouts/nuthatch.png", "w": 532, "h": 327, "len": 372.0, "beak": [0.79, 0.42, "R"], "mask": {"c": 32, "r": 20, "b": "fgAAAH8AAAB/AAAAP4AAAD+AAAAfwAAAH/4gAB///gAf///AB///gAf//gAD//wAA//4AAP/8AAB//AAAf/AAAD+AAAA/gAAAP4AAAB/AAA="}}, {"name": "Tufted Titmouse", "sci": "Baeolophus bicolor", "n": 1, "first": "9:05 AM", "last": "9:05 AM", "ts": 1793801121, "mins": [545], "anim": null, "trunk": false, "fact": "Titmice line their nests with hair, and they have been seen plucking it from live raccoons, dogs, and even people.", "vox": "sounds/voice/baeolophus-bicolor.mp3?v=1793112971", "snd": "sounds/baeolophus-bicolor.mp3?v=1793113083", "sndby": "Jim Berry, xeno-canto XC649331, CC BY-NC-SA 4.0", "key": "titmouse", "cm": 16.5, "img": "cutouts/titmouse.png", "w": 574, "h": 800, "len": 653.0, "beak": [0.2, 0.25, "L"], "mask": {"c": 32, "r": 45, "b": "cAAAAHAAAAAwAAAAIAAAAGgDAAB8B4AAPB8AAD5/AAA+/4AAeP+AAHv/wAB7/8AA+P/gAPhn4ACbH/AADz/wAA9/+AAPf/wABb/8AB+//gAO//4ABt//AAfP/wAHx/+AB+P/gAPh/8AHiP/AB47/wAeH/+AHofvgBvOB8AD3APgAfAB4APgAPAD4AB4A+AAfAPwADwA3AA8APwAHAD4AAAAfAAAAGwAAABgAAAAMAAAADAAA"}}, {"name": "Northern Flicker", "sci": "Colaptes auratus", "n": 5, "first": "9:06 AM", "last": "9:11 AM", "ts": 1793801195, "mins": [546, 546, 548, 550, 551], "anim": null, "trunk": false, "fact": "A woodpecker that often feeds on the ground, mainly on ants. Eastern flickers flash bright yellow under the wings and tail when they fly.", "vox": "sounds/voice/colaptes-auratus.mp3?v=1793112986", "snd": "sounds/colaptes-auratus.mp3?v=1793113157", "sndby": "Thomas Magarian, xeno-canto XC354617, CC BY-NC-SA 4.0", "key": "flicker", "cm": 31.0, "img": "cutouts/flicker.png", "w": 800, "h": 579, "len": 732.0, "beak": [0.66, 0.07, "R"], "mask": {"c": 32, "r": 23, "b": "AA/AAAAP/AAAH/gAAD/AAAB/gAEA/4AHAf+ADgH/wDwD/4DwA/+B4Af/h4AH/x4AD/54AA//4AAf/4AAH98AAD79AAA+8AAAP/AAAH8gAAD+AAAA+AAAAPAAAAA="}}, {"name": "Carolina Wren", "sci": "Thryothorus ludovicianus", "n": 1, "first": "9:09 AM", "last": "9:09 AM", "ts": 1793801386, "mins": [549], "anim": null, "trunk": false, "fact": "Its song sounds like “teakettle, teakettle, teakettle,” and pairs often sing duets. It will nest in odd places: flowerpots, mailboxes, even old boots.", "vox": "sounds/voice/thryothorus-ludovicianus.mp3?v=1793113060", "snd": "sounds/thryothorus-ludovicianus.mp3?v=1793113075", "sndby": "Jacob Saucier, xeno-canto XC498646, CC BY-NC-SA 4.0", "key": "wren", "cm": 13.0, "img": "cutouts/wren.png", "w": 800, "h": 633, "len": 781.0, "beak": [0.85, 0.21, "R"], "mask": {"c": 32, "r": 25, "b": "OAAAADgAAAA8AAAAHgB+AB4B/+APA//wB5//AAf//gAH//4AB//+AAf//AAH//wAD//4AAP/+AAB//H/AP/f/wA///8AGf/cAB/7AA//8AAP/4AAP/8AAP9gAAD8AAAAcAAAAA=="}}, {"name": "Blue Jay", "sci": "Cyanocitta cristata", "n": 2, "first": "9:11 AM", "last": "9:35 AM", "ts": 1793801510, "mins": [551, 575], "anim": null, "trunk": false, "fact": "That blue isn’t a pigment. Tiny structures in the feathers scatter light, which is why a crushed blue feather looks brown. Jays also bury acorns and help spread oak trees.", "vox": "sounds/voice/cyanocitta-cristata.mp3?v=1793112993", "snd": "sounds/cyanocitta-cristata.mp3?v=1793113328", "sndby": "Christopher McPherson, xeno-canto XC691163, CC BY-NC-SA 4.0", "key": "bluejay", "cm": 28.0, "img": "cutouts/bluejay.png", "w": 385, "h": 369, "len": 474.0, "beak": [0.99, 0.17, "R"], "mask": {"c": 32, "r": 31, "b": "AAAPgAAAD+AAAA/4AAAH+AAAD/8AAB//AAA//gAAf/AAAP/wAAH/8AAD//AAB//wAB//4AAf/+AAf//gAH//zAD//5wA//94Af+OcAP/8eAD9/fgA/l/gAPgHwAHgD4AHwA4AB8AeAA+AGAAfADgAHgAwADwAAAA4AAAAA=="}}, {"name": "Dark-eyed Junco", "sci": "Junco hyemalis", "n": 1, "first": "9:17 AM", "last": "9:17 AM", "ts": 1793801831, "mins": [557], "anim": null, "trunk": false, "fact": "Often called “snowbirds” because they show up as winter arrives. The white outer tail feathers flash as they flit away.", "vox": "sounds/voice/junco-hyemalis.mp3?v=1793113009", "snd": "sounds/junco-hyemalis.mp3?v=1793113154", "sndby": "Phoenix Birder, xeno-canto XC563632, CC BY-NC-SA 4.0", "key": "junco", "cm": 15.5, "img": "cutouts/junco.png", "w": 800, "h": 562, "len": 625.0, "beak": [0.2, 0.22, "L"], "mask": {"c": 32, "r": 22, "b": "AAAABgAAAAYA8AAMAfgAOAP8ADAD/gBxAf8AZwH/gMwB/8G4Af/j8AD/94AA//4AAH/8AAA//gAAH/4AAAf/AAQPh4AEPwHABfgA4A/QAHA+AAAA8AAAAA=="}}, {"name": "Eastern Bluebird", "sci": "Sialia sialis", "n": 1, "first": "9:17 AM", "last": "9:17 AM", "ts": 1793801873, "mins": [557], "anim": null, "trunk": false, "fact": "Bluebirds nest in tree cavities, and their numbers crashed in the early 1900s when starlings and house sparrows took the holes. Volunteer nest-box trails helped them rebound.", "vox": "sounds/voice/sialia-sialis.mp3?v=1793113046", "snd": "sounds/sialia-sialis.mp3?v=1793113130", "sndby": "Robin Carter, xeno-canto XC1374, CC BY-NC-ND 2.5", "key": "bluebird", "cm": 18.0, "img": "cutouts/bluebird.png", "w": 800, "h": 609, "len": 744.0, "beak": [0.69, 0.1, "R"], "mask": {"c": 32, "r": 24, "b": "AAPwAAAH/AAAD/4AAA/4AAAf8AAAH/AAAD/wAAB/+AAAf/gDAP/wDgH/8BwD//B4A//h4Af/54AH/94AD/98AB//4AA/P4AAPH0AAHPhAABvgAAA3gAAAPgAAABwAAAA"}}, {"name": "Downy Woodpecker", "sci": "Dryobates pubescens", "n": 5, "first": "9:18 AM", "last": "11:01 AM", "ts": 1793801922, "mins": [558, 558, 559, 565, 661], "anim": null, "trunk": false, "fact": "North America’s smallest woodpecker. It is small enough to forage on thin weed stalks and twigs that bigger woodpeckers can’t cling to.", "vox": "sounds/voice/dryobates-pubescens.mp3?v=1793112995", "snd": "sounds/dryobates-pubescens.mp3?v=1793113092", "sndby": "Thomas G. Graves, xeno-canto XC89855, CC BY-NC-ND 2.5", "key": "downy", "cm": 16.0, "img": "cutouts/downy.png", "w": 486, "h": 800, "len": 707.0, "beak": [0.59, 0.1, "L"], "mask": {"c": 32, "r": 53, "b": "AAAAAP/4AAD//AAA//wAAP/8IAD//DwA//wf4H/8D/x//Af+f/wH/3/8A/9//AP/f/wD/3/8B/9//A//f/wf/n/8P/x//H/8f/7//H/+//x////8f////D////x////8f////H////h////4f///+D////A////wP///4D///+A////gP///wD///8A///+AP///gD///wA///4AP//+AD///AA///wAP//sAD//4AA//8AAP//AAD//wAA//4AAP/+AAD//gAA//4AAP/8AAD//AAA="}}, {"name": "Rusty Blackbird", "sci": "Euphagus carolinus", "n": 5, "first": "9:19 AM", "last": "9:46 AM", "ts": 1793801943, "mins": [559, 560, 560, 571, 586], "anim": null, "trunk": false, "fact": "Rusty Blackbirds have undergone one of the steepest declines of any North American landbird, and scientists are still working out why. They forage in wet woods, flipping leaves along streams.", "vox": "sounds/voice/euphagus-carolinus.mp3?v=1793113002", "snd": "sounds/euphagus-carolinus.mp3?v=1793113205", "sndby": "Todd Wilson, xeno-canto XC63365, CC BY-NC-ND 2.5", "key": "rustyblackbird", "cm": 23.0, "img": "cutouts/rustyblackbird.png", "w": 800, "h": 602, "len": 706.0, "beak": [0.44, 0.1, "R"], "mask": {"c": 32, "r": 24, "b": "A/AAAAf8AAAH/AAAB/AAAA/4AAAP/AADD/4ADg//ABwP/4B4B//A4Af/w8AH/+8AA//8AAH/8AAA//gAAH/8AAA+/AAA/j4AAfAfAAfgD8AewAfAOAADwPAAAADgAAAA"}}, {"name": "American Goldfinch", "sci": "Spinus tristis", "n": 4, "first": "9:19 AM", "last": "9:45 AM", "ts": 1793801950, "mins": [559, 559, 575, 585], "anim": null, "trunk": false, "fact": "One of the strictest vegetarians among birds. Goldfinches nest late in summer, when thistle seeds are ripe, and swap bright yellow for olive-brown in winter.", "vox": "sounds/voice/spinus-tristis.mp3?v=1793113053", "snd": "sounds/spinus-tristis.mp3?v=1793113090", "sndby": "Will Sweet, xeno-canto XC453934, CC BY-NC-SA 4.0", "key": "goldfinch", "cm": 12.0, "img": "cutouts/goldfinch.png", "w": 517, "h": 369, "len": 415.0, "beak": [0.18, 0.15, "L"], "mask": {"c": 32, "r": 23, "b": "APgAAAH4AAAD/AAAB/wAAAP+AAEB/wAHAf+ADgH/wBwB/8DwAf/h4AH/94AA//8AAH/8ABA/+AAAH/wAAB/+AAA/fhAAeA8AA/gHgAfgB8APAANAPAAAADgAAAA="}}, {"name": "American Crow", "sci": "Corvus brachyrhynchos", "n": 1, "first": "9:29 AM", "last": "9:29 AM", "ts": 1793802574, "mins": [569], "anim": null, "trunk": false, "fact": "Crows remember individual human faces for years, and they seem to pass the grudge along to other crows.", "vox": "sounds/voice/corvus-brachyrhynchos.mp3?v=1793112990", "snd": "sounds/corvus-brachyrhynchos.mp3?v=1793113335", "sndby": "David Vander Pluym, xeno-canto XC289011, CC BY-NC-SA 4.0", "key": "crow", "cm": 45.0, "img": "cutouts/crow.png", "w": 553, "h": 364, "len": 568.0, "beak": [0.94, 0.14, "R"], "mask": {"c": 32, "r": 21, "b": "AAAHwAAAD/gAAB/+AAB//gAD/+AAD//AAB//wAB//4AB//+AB///AAf//wAP//4AH//4AH//8AD//8AA/x+AAPgPwAbwF/6/f////3///7Bhw5AA"}}, {"name": "Northern Cardinal", "sci": "Cardinalis cardinalis", "n": 1, "first": "11:29 AM", "last": "11:29 AM", "ts": 1793809789, "mins": [689], "anim": null, "trunk": false, "fact": "Female cardinals sing too, which is unusual for a North American songbird. During courtship the male feeds her seeds beak to beak.", "vox": "sounds/voice/cardinalis-cardinalis.mp3?v=1793112978", "snd": "sounds/cardinalis-cardinalis.mp3?v=1793113085", "sndby": "David Tattersley, xeno-canto XC1000248, CC BY-NC-SA 4.0", "key": "cardinal", "cm": 22.0, "img": "cutouts/cardinal.png", "w": 516, "h": 348, "len": 530.0, "beak": [0.06, 0.28, "L"], "mask": {"c": 32, "r": 22, "b": "AYAAAAeAAAAPgAAAH4AAAB/AAAA/4AAAf/gAAB/+AAAf/4AAH//AAA//8AAP//gAB//8AAf//AAD//+AAf//wEB/v/jwf3v+///+/z//vn945gA+MAAAAA=="}}], "also": [], "date": "Wednesday, November 4", "where": "", "updated": "12:25 PM", "sun": null, "code": "3bb51669b7", "listen": {"state": "on", "since": null, "control": false}}